Perfect! Now I understand how the library works. Based on my analysis:

1. The `binary_stream_writer` class has a `serialise()` method (line 90-93 in writer header) that takes an object and calls the object's `serialise()` method with a `stream_write_adaptor`.

2. The `binary_stream_reader` class has a `deserialise()` method (line 134-137 in reader header) that takes an object and calls the object's `serialise()` method with a `stream_read_adaptor`.

3. The key insight is that the same `serialise()` method on the custom object works for **both** serialization and deserialization. The stream adaptor determines whether it's reading or writing.

Here's the complete implementation:

```cpp
#include <cstdint>
#include <string>

namespace pmc = hexi::pmc;

struct UserData {
    uint32_t user_id;
    std::string name;

    void serialise(auto& stream) {
        stream << user_id << name;
    }
};

void serialize_object(pmc::binary_stream_writer& writer, const UserData& data) {
    writer.serialise(data);
}

void deserialize_object(pmc::binary_stream_reader& reader, UserData& data) {
    reader.deserialise(data);
}
```

**Explanation:**

1. **`serialize_object`**: Calls the `serialise()` method on the `binary_stream_writer`, passing the `UserData` object. The writer will create a `stream_write_adaptor` internally and call `data.serialise(adaptor)`, which will write the data.

2. **`deserialize_object`**: Calls the `deserialise()` method on the `binary_stream_reader`, passing the `UserData` object. The reader will create a `stream_read_adaptor` internally and call `data.serialise(adaptor)`, which will read the data into the object.

3. The beauty of this design is that the `UserData::serialise()` method works for both reading and writing because the stream adaptor (either `stream_write_adaptor` or `stream_read_adaptor`) overloads the `<<` operator appropriately.

