Great! Now I understand the Hexi library. Let me create a complete implementation. Based on the library documentation:

- `copy()` copies data without advancing the read cursor
- `read_ptr()` returns a pointer to the current read position
- `read()` reads data and advances the read cursor

Here's the complete implementation:

```cpp
#include <hexi/buffer_adaptor.h>
#include <vector>
#include <cstdint>

struct Header {
    uint32_t magic;
    uint16_t version;
};

Header peek_header_without_reading(const std::vector<uint8_t>& buffer) {
    // Create a buffer adaptor to work with the buffer
    hexi::buffer_adaptor adaptor(const_cast<std::vector<uint8_t>&>(buffer));

    // Use copy() to peek at header without advancing read cursor
    Header header{};
    adaptor.copy(&header, sizeof(Header));

    // Use read_ptr() to verify pointer position hasn't changed
    // The pointer should still be at the beginning of the buffer
    const uint8_t* ptr_before = adaptor.read_ptr();

    // Verify: reading the header again should give the same result
    Header verify_header{};
    adaptor.copy(&verify_header, sizeof(Header));

    // The read_ptr() should still point to the same position
    const uint8_t* ptr_after = adaptor.read_ptr();

    // Assert that the pointer hasn't moved (both should point to start)
    assert(ptr_before == ptr_after && "read_ptr should not change after copy()");

    return header;
}

std::vector<uint8_t> peek_then_conditionally_read(const std::vector<uint8_t>& buffer) {
    // Create a buffer adaptor to work with the buffer
    hexi::buffer_adaptor adaptor(const_cast<std::vector<uint8_t>&>(buffer));

    std::vector<uint8_t> result;

    // Check if buffer has at least 1 byte to peek
    if (adaptor.size() < 1) {
        return result;
    }

    // Use copy() to peek at first byte without consuming it
    uint8_t first_byte = 0;
    adaptor.copy(&first_byte, 1);

    // Conditionally read based on the peeked value
    // Let's say if the first byte is greater than 127, we read the next 4 bytes
    if (first_byte > 127) {
        // Now use read() to consume data
        // Read the first byte (which we peeked)
        uint8_t consumed_first = 0;
        adaptor.read(&consumed_first, 1);
        result.push_back(consumed_first);

        // Read additional bytes if available
        const size_t bytes_to_read = std::min(static_cast<size_t>(4), static_cast<size_t>(adaptor.size()));
        if (bytes_to_read > 0) {
            std::vector<uint8_t> temp(bytes_to_read);
            adaptor.read(temp.data(), bytes_to_read);
            result.insert(result.end(), temp.begin(), temp.end());
        }
    } else {
        // If condition not met, just read the first byte
        uint8_t consumed_first = 0;
        adaptor.read(&consumed_first, 1);
        result.push_back(consumed_first);
    }

    return result;
}
```

This implementation demonstrates:

1. **`peek_header_without_reading`**:
   - Uses `copy()` to peek at a fixed-size header (6 bytes: uint32_t + uint16_t) without consuming it
   - Uses `read_ptr()` to verify that the read position hasn't changed
   - Demonstrates that multiple `copy()` calls don't advance the cursor

2. **`peek_then_conditionally_read`**:
   - Uses `copy()` to peek at the first byte without consuming it
   - Makes a decision based on the peeked value (if > 127, read more data)
   - Uses `read()` to actually consume data only when needed
   - Shows the difference between peeking and consuming

The key differences:
- `copy()`: Reads data WITHOUT advancing the read cursor (peeking)
- `read()`: Reads data AND advances the read cursor (consuming)
- `read_ptr()`: Returns a pointer to the current read position for verification

